Z||000004||fieroshop||10-31-1999||09:06 AM||||If you are going to spend that much money in trying to get all those ponies out of a V6, you are better off going to a V8 and building that up. The V6 is cost prohibitive||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero||10-31-1999||12:04 PM||||To get a 560HP on a 3.1 hmmmm...I think you will need a basket underneath the cradle. To get that much HP and be reliable...like everybody else says ...go with a V8 kit. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||olympic||10-31-1999||04:46 PM||||The th-125 was rated to handle about about 180 lbs of torque and maybe up to 250 if driven carefully. There would be no way to modify it to handle 500+ hp. The only oem trany that would even come close would be the Cadillac/Aurora 4T80E which is rated for 400 lbs of torque. ||||reg||3
Z||000007||Eric Marsh||11-01-1999||10:48 AM||||Archie at V8Archie says a stock 125 can handle up to 300 hp if not abused. I've got a 125 that was modified by Jim's Performance Transmission, the guy who does tranny work for Archie. Jim told me that he thinks it will hold up to my 450ft/lb of torque and 450-475 hp with a good cooler and regular maintance. <P>As for 500+ horsepower out of a 3.1, why bother. Even if you can get that kind of power its going to be extremely high maintance, requiring major engine work on a regular basis. <P>If you want a high horsepower V6, go for the 3.8 turbo buick. They are designed to take it. Kenny-Bell is getting 600-800 horses and even more out of a turbo buick. The other option is a V8 conversion. A smallblock chevy will give you as much power as you can afford and drivable torque too.<P>Eric||||reg||1
